# `will` puts the system's present behavior into the future
# (`running make will build the binary`), and the reader has to work
# out whether the sentence describes a later event or the system's
# normal behavior.
# The technical-writing skill writes the present tense for what the
# system does and keeps `will` for a later event, which a word rule
# cannot tell apart, so the rule is a suggestion.
#
# The filter narrows the finding to `will`, so a rule that reports the
# words after it keeps its own finding (`will give up`, `will be
# created`). A sentence that opens with `Will` is a question, and the
# filter needs a word before `will`, so it passes. The guard skips `we
# will`, which the signpost rule reports (`we will explore`), and
# `time will tell`, which the closing-cliche rule reports. It also
# skips `will want`, which the obligation rule reports, and `will be
# added`, `will be supported`, `will be removed`, and `will be
# deprecated`, which the roadmap rule reports, and `will be able to`,
# which the wordy rule reports. prose-lint turns the rule off for a
# commit message, because a commit message describes what the system
# does after the change lands.
#
# It hit 1447 lines in the kubernetes, nix.dev, and Go docs, and 10 of
# 10 sampled hits described the system's normal behavior.
expr skip [we, time]
expr roadmap [added, supported, removed, deprecated]
expr main <(!@skip will [<VERB, !want>, (be ![@roadmap, able])]), will>
let message "Future tense. Write the present tense for what the system does."
let description "Flags will before a verb, where the present tense usually describes what the system does."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Running make builds the binary."
allows "Will the job retry?"
allows "We will explore the scheduler."
allows "Only time will tell."
allows "The flag will be removed in the next release."
allows "You will want to pin the version."
allows "The caller will be able to cancel the request."
